ing between layers 28 and 40. Looking at the performance metrics, Llama 4 Scout achieves an English AUROC of 0.81, which trails Qwen3 but substantially outperforms the baseline Llama-3.1-8B at 0.728. The Pareto frontier shows weak negative correlation (around -0.33), continuing the pattern from the earlier model where certain languages like Russian, Korean, and Arabic maintain or slightly improve performance with depth, while others like Swahili, Yoruba, and Amharic degrade significantly—likely because the MoE routing allocates different experts to different languages at deeper layers, creating heterogeneous per-language behavior.\n\nThe overall Pareto strength remains weaker than Qwen3, which aligns with the best-tier expectation of more structured representations being more evident in Qwen. Three low-resource languages (Yoruba, Burmese, Amharic) fall below the 0.60 threshold, though the transfer success rate of 17 out of 20 languages (0.85) meets the best-tier minimum. Now I'm examining the detailed Pareto frontier summary across the 20 languages and 12 layers, looking at the zero-shot AUROC values and correlation patterns for each language family. Now I'm looking at the figure descriptions and analysis for the Llama 4 results — comparing its Pareto frontier against Qwen3, where Llama shows a much flatter slope and higher heterogeneity across languages, with some languages like Russian and Korean trending upward while others like Swahili and Yoruba decline.\n\nMoving into the CaCLR implementation evaluation across both architectures and twenty languages, examining the hyperparameter sweep results...\n\nGenerating evaluation metrics... Now I'm looking at the statistical significance of the beta parameter ablation with a p-value of 0.003 and a Cohen's d of 0.52, then examining how CaCLR's improvements vary across different resource tiers—showing stronger gains on lower-tier resources—and noting that at the English-optimal layer the model achieves even better perform