across languages at around 12°, much smaller than the global representation angle of 39°—a ratio of 3.15 that suggests this direction captures a more consistent cross-linguistic structure than overall representations. The presence or absence of CKA cliffs explains the architectural differences in Pareto efficiency between models, which is a significant discovery. Now I'm looking at how the number of parallel pairs affects linear projection performance across different sample sizes. Looking at the per-architecture deltas, Llama shows similar gains to Qwen at 50 pairs, and comparing against CaCLR, a linear projection with 50 pairs achieves roughly what their method does with 35 pairs. The core insight is that we're learning a rotation to align the target language's correctness subspace with English's, which is inherently low-dimensional—a 1-2D transformation that 50 pairs sufficiently constrains, explaining why gains plateau so sharply beyond that point and why 10 pairs remains unstable.\n\nGenerating markdown table... The two approaches complement each other—linear projection needs labeled parallel pairs but outperforms CaCLR, while CaCLR only requires parallel inputs without labels. Now I'm planning the full-scale probing control tasks across 20 languages and two architectures, examining selectivity metrics and how low-selectivity cells distribute across early layers and low-resource languages. The control AUROC staying near 0.50 confirms the probe isn't picking up the permuted labels, so the low selectivity in early layers and low-resource languages reflects genuine signal gaps rather than artifacts. About 10% of cells show capacity-related distortions, leaving roughly 90% of results as authentic correctness signals, though I should flag the early-layer, low-resource problem zone in the paper as a methodological caveat.\n\nNow I'm looking at the selectivity distribution across depth ranges and resource levels, which shows a clear gradient where selectivity increase